      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Mia, why are flamingos pink?",
      "board": {
        "type": "title",
        "left": {
          "icon": "🦩",
          "label": "Flamingo"
        },
        "right": {
          "icon": "💗",
          "label": "Pink?"
        },
        "mid": "?"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Because of what they eat! Tiny shrimp and little water plants called algae.",
      "punch": "eat",
      "board": {
        "type": "gallery",
        "title": "Flamingo food",
        "items": [
          {
            "icon": "img:Animals/DBCLS/Japanese_tiger_prawn",
            "label": "Tiny shrimp",
            "cue": "shrimp"
          },
          {
            "icon": "🌿",
            "label": "Algae",
            "cue": "algae"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "And the food makes them pink?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Yes! That food has natural colours, like the orange in carrots. The colours go into their feathers and turn them pink.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🥕",
        "text": "Colours from food",
        "cue": "colours",
        "sub": "Into the feathers!",
        "sub_cue": "feathers"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "Are baby flamingos pink too?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Nope! Baby flamingos are fluffy and grey. They turn pink as they grow up and eat that food.",
      "board": {
        "type": "compare",
        "left": {
          "label": "Baby",
          "items": [
            {
              "icon": "🐣",
              "text": "Fluffy and grey",
              "cue": "grey"
            }
          ]
        },
        "right": {
          "label": "Grown-up",
          "items": [
            {
              "icon": "🦩",
              "text": "Pink feathers",
              "cue": "pink"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "What if a flamingo ate different food?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Its feathers would get paler. That's why zoos give flamingos special food, so they stay pink.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🦩",
        "text": "Less colour, paler bird",
        "cue": "paler",
        "sub": "Zoo food keeps them pink",
        "sub_cue": "zoos"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "How do they eat with that funny bent beak?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "They dip their heads upside down. Their beak works like a strainer, keeping food in and letting water out.",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🙃",
        "text": "Upside-down eating!",
        "cue": "upside",
        "sub": "Beak = a strainer",
        "sub_cue": "strainer"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "And why do they stand on one leg?"
    },
    {
      "who": "MIA",
      "text": "Scientists think it may help them stay warm or save energy. It's still a bit of a mystery!",
      "punch": "mystery",
      "board": {
        "type": "big",
        "icon": "🤔",
        "text": "A one-leg mystery",
        "cue": "mystery",
        "sub": "Maybe to stay warm",
        "sub_cue": "warm"
      }
    },
    {
      "who": "LEO",
      "text": "So flamingos are pink because of the shrimp and algae they eat!",
      "board": {
        "type": "summary",
